    "changelog": [
      "When to Segment: added a short, explicit list of recurring risky surface patterns (preposition-led coordinations; exclusives followed by contrast/negation; ambiguous verbs without their object; noun + locative/postmodifier; symmetric 'called ... and called ...' parallels; verb+pronoun+PP sequences). This records the candidate_rules succinctly and keeps them as candidates rather than forbidding them.",
      "Priority Rules: kept the existing confidence tiers but added a concise 'Specific demotion signals' subsection that merges all candidate_rules into ranked risk factors to be applied when ordering candidates (prefer boundary after conjunction/second coordinated noun, wait for contrast after exclusives, require object for ambiguous verbs, prefer after full locative/postmodifier, keep 'called ...' parallels intact, wait for pronoun+PP to appear).",
      "Decision Procedure: made ranking prescriptive by describing how to convert the demotion signals into an ordered confidence ranking (compute a simple risk score by accumulating demotion factors, then sort candidates). This implements the candidate_rules operationally so risky positions remain candidates but are ranked lower."
    ],
    "next_changelog": [
      "Strengthened [Core Principles] with concise, surface-form guidance about the common mechanisms that cause premature boundaries (coordinated participant lists, locative/complementive nouns, ambiguous verbs lacking objects, exclusives like 'only' with contrast, pronouns inside verb phrases, and noun heads awaiting 'of'/'relative' postmodifiers). This gives the model clear, language-surface cues to lower confidence without changing candidate marking or adding categorical bans.",
      "Replaced two example items (one in the 'Segment' set and one in the 'Do NOT Segment' set) to illustrate the specific risky patterns (coordinated participants and ambiguous verb/object situations). The new examples show how to mark candidates generously but rank risky splits lower or avoid them when they would break needed complements."
    ]
  },
